{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices across the full 30-day horizon (often by ~8–15 EUR), especially during downtrends.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: the model fails to capture regime shifts from rising to falling; it continues projecting elevated levels after the peak, implying insufficient responsiveness to momentum reversal.",
    "Underestimates drawdown magnitude: when the market sells off, the forecast path declines too slowly and not far enough (needs stronger negative slope / larger cumulative drop).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following late-history highs (~82–84), the model projects rebounds/continuation to mid/high-80s that do not materialize; cap upside extensions after recent spikes and favor mean reversion.",
    "Horizon error structure: near-term (days 1–5) errors are smaller but still upward-biased; medium/long horizon (days 10–30) errors widen materially—apply an increasing downward adjustment with forecast horizon.",
    "Mean-reversion anchor too high: the model’s implied equilibrium level is above the realized post-peak trading range (~68–73); re-anchor forecasts toward the recent realized range when volatility rises and direction turns negative."
  ]
}
